# Using Workers (Subagents) to Split Tasks

Use the **worker/subagent** feature to split work across separate agents when it makes the response faster or more thorough.

## When to launch a worker

- **Parallel work**: Multiple independent steps (e.g. explore web codebase in one place + run mobile tests in another). Launch 2–4 workers in parallel when steps don't depend on each other.
- **Broad exploration**: "How does X work in web?", "Where is Y defined?", "What's the structure of Z?" — use an **explore** worker with thoroughness "quick", "medium", or "very thorough".
- **Shell/commands**: `eas build`, `bun install`, native compile, EAS Submit — use a **shell** worker so the main agent stays focused on code/design.
- **Research or multi-step work**: Deep search of web codebase for parity reference, multi-file refactors, follow-up steps — use a **generalPurpose** worker with a clear, self-contained task description.

## When not to use a worker

- **Single, narrow question** (e.g. "Where is `useAuth` defined?") — use grep/read directly
- **Quick, few-step tasks** — handle in the main turn
- **Tasks that need your current context** — don't delegate if the worker wouldn't have enough info

## How to use

- **One message, multiple workers**: Prefer one user turn with several worker launches (e.g. explore + shell) when tasks are independent.
- **Describe the task fully**: Workers don't see the rest of the chat; put all needed context (paths, goals, constraints) in the worker prompt.
- **Say what you want back**: Specify what the worker should return (e.g. "list of file paths", "summary of how auth works on web") so you can use it in the main reply.

## Mobile-specific worker patterns

- **Parity check**: launch an explore worker to read the web equivalent of the feature you're implementing on mobile, while you set up the RN screen structure in the main agent
- **EAS build monitoring**: launch a shell worker to `eas build` while you continue on the next ticket in the main agent
- **Storybook screenshots for visual regression**: shell worker for the screenshot capture, main agent for the code under test

Split tasks across workers when it clearly improves speed or quality; otherwise keep the work in the main agent.
